    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nMaserati Celebrates the Ghibli's 60th Anniversary With Special Ghibli SS Classiche Certification\n9 photos\nPhoto: Maserati\nAlthough the Ghibli is no more, Maserati has found an interesting way to celebrate one of its most celebrated nameplates. Instead of commissioning a one-off build or coming up with a new concept, the Stellantis-controlled Italian manufacturer has turned its attention to a 1970 model that the Maserati family has kept in its own collection. The celebratory vehicle is listed as AM115/49*1948* by its maker.\nMarking 60 years since the 2-seater concept was unveiled at the Turin Motor Show in November 2026, this model has recently passed through the automaker's classic vehicle certification process. AM115/49*1948* left Modena on November 25, 1970, and its first owner specified Oro Longchamps paintwork.\nToday, said coupe wears fresh Giallo Fly paintwork. The cabin retains the original black leather upholstery, adding to the specialness of this VIN. Under the long and beautifully sculpted hood, the Giorgetto Giugiaro-penned fastback is rocking the larger 4.9-liter naturally aspirated V8 instead of the lesser 4.7-liter unit.\nThe Ghibli SS motor is related to the 4.7-liter mill, and this engine was key to creating the fastest Maserati road car at the time. Its top speed, according to Maserati, is 280 kilometers per hour (nearly 174 miles per hour). SS chassis numbers include the /49 designation, whereas AM115 stands for Ghibli.\nMaserati produced the first generation in limited numbers through 1973, and the AM in AM115 designates a founding Maserati brother. Alfieri Maserati was the driving force behind the company. Unfortunately, this Maserati brother never fully recovered after a horrific 1927 racing accident. Alfieri died in 1932, at just 44 years old, from surgical complications while attempting to save his remaining kidney.\nPhoto: Maserati A serious grand tourer by 1970 standards, the Ghibli SS combined the ability to cover long distances at considerable speed with the comfort and quality expected from an expensive Italian coupe. Designed by Giugiaro during his tenure at Ghia, the Ghibli used dry-sump lubrication because Maserati wanted to mount its V8 as low as possible in the chassis.\nThe dry-sump lubrication system partly explains the design of the hood, with Giugiaro creating a very sleek front end with a remarkably flat and low hood. Commercial deliveries kicked off in 1967, and two years later, Maserati expanded the range with the Ghibli Spyder.\nFewer than 1,300 vehicles were produced in total, of which the Spyder accounted for 125. The SS coupe stopped production after 425 units, making the Maserati family-owned Ghibli SS all the more special. Beyond the Italian Republic, the Ghibli attracted plenty of attention from well-to-do peeps.\nHenry Ford II comes to mind, for he bought a Ghibli that was displayed in the lobby of the Ford Motor Company's Development Center in Dearborn. Not exactly the Italian Riviera, but hey, it's something! Today's GranTurismo and GranCabrio both carry the first-generation Maserati Ghibli's philosophy into a different era, one that includes twin-turbo sixers and full-electric muscle.\nThe base GranTurismo is a very fast machine due to the all-wheel drive and a nicely calibrated ZF automatic box, with Maserati claiming 3.7 seconds to 60 miles per hour (97 kilometers per hour). Upgrading (or downgrading, depending on how you see electrification) to the Folgore specification means 751 zero-emission ponies and 2.6 seconds to 60 miles per hour. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Maserati (@maserati)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nmaserati ghibli maserati classic car v8 grand tourer\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT The c.fold Transformable Travel Trailer Is Designed to Be Different in Every Way",
